X:1
T:I Plant Some Rice
T:Ilocos Sur action song (historical English schoolbook version)
C:traditional (Ilocos Sur); notation by Florencio Buado
O:Philippines (Ilocos Sur, Ilocano)
R:children's action song
S:Norberto Romualdez et al., The Philippine Progressive Music Series for the Primary Grades, Silver Burdett, digitized volume revised through 1948, p. 25, "I Plant Some Rice," notation by Florencio Buado; HathiTrust Public Domain
N:An action song the book calls popular among the Ilocano people, about the year's rice work: planting, harvesting, pounding, cooking and eating. Mime the work, without tools, on the first beat of bars 2, 3, 7 and 8 (counting the pickup as bar 1), a new action in each verse. Present rice work as skilled labour.
N:The book prints only these English school verses, with no Ilocano title or words, so this is the schoolbook's version, not necessarily the song as Ilocano families sing it; ask an Ilocano speaker about the words if you can. The tempo is ours. Voice and actions only: the book prints no accompaniment.
M:2/4
L:1/8
Q:1/4=104
K:G
